// FAIL canary (float semantics F1, 2026-07-18): a `Wrapping` policy domain on
// a float primitive is a hard compile error -- there is no modular reading of
// a float (ch5 "Float Facts"; the same principle rejects `in Wrapping` on a
// float source in the float-to-int cast). Before F1 this SILENTLY no-opped:
// `f32 in Wrapping` type-checked and both engines ran plain IEEE, so the
// declared policy was a lie. Saturating/Trapping are real float policies pinned
// by the F5 pass canaries; integer `in Wrapping` stays legal (decision 17).
use omega::language::std::console;
data Main {
    console: Console;
    speed: f32 in Wrapping;
}
machine Main::main(&mut self) {
    self.console.exit_process(0);
}
